\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Study design and a deployed agent.} \textbf  {a}, Corrupted values were injected between the database and the agent, in a tool layer or, for the deployed agent, an intercepting proxy; the agent's answer and concerns were scored. Bottom: the forms a corrupted record took (illustrative values). \textbf  {b}, Biomni with live UniProt and PubChem APIs: share of corrupted runs in which the value was relayed, caught, or never requested (relayed/delivered at right). \textbf  {c}, Relay of plausible values by field (shaded rows, compounds; filled circles, fields counted as having an exact derivation route (Methods); open diamonds, other fields), with 95\% Wilson intervals; numbers give relayed/delivered runs.}}{19}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:deploy}{{1}{19}{\textbf {Study design and a deployed agent.} \textbf {a}, Corrupted values were injected between the database and the agent, in a tool layer or, for the deployed agent, an intercepting proxy; the agent's answer and concerns were scored. Bottom: the forms a corrupted record took (illustrative values). \textbf {b}, Biomni with live UniProt and PubChem APIs: share of corrupted runs in which the value was relayed, caught, or never requested (relayed/delivered at right). \textbf {c}, Relay of plausible values by field (shaded rows, compounds; filled circles, fields counted as having an exact derivation route (Methods); open diamonds, other fields), with 95\% Wilson intervals; numbers give relayed/delivered runs}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Noticed yet answered.} Share of runs in which each model relayed the corrupted value into its final answer, for impossible and plausible values in the protein (\textbf  {a}, \textbf  {b}) and chemistry (\textbf  {c}, \textbf  {d}) benchmarks. Dark segments: relayed runs in which the agent also raised a concern; light segments: relayed without a concern; black ticks: share of all runs raising a concern. Numbers give the relay rate (\%).}}{20}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:noticed}{{2}{20}{\textbf {Noticed yet answered.} Share of runs in which each model relayed the corrupted value into its final answer, for impossible and plausible values in the protein (\textbf {a}, \textbf {b}) and chemistry (\textbf {c}, \textbf {d}) benchmarks. Dark segments: relayed runs in which the agent also raised a concern; light segments: relayed without a concern; black ticks: share of all runs raising a concern. Numbers give the relay rate (\%)}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Override boundaries.} Share of runs relaying a corrupted value as the corruption grows, with the full record available and the paired field scaled alike, for proteins (\textbf  {a}, value decreased; \textbf  {b}, increased) and molecules (\textbf  {c}, decreased; \textbf  {d}, increased). Bands: 95\% Wilson intervals (30--40 tasks per point after excluding runs without a parseable answer). Strips below each panel: fitted factor at which half of runs relay the value, with its 95\% interval; estimates outside the tested range are given as numbers.}}{21}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:boundary}{{3}{21}{\textbf {Override boundaries.} Share of runs relaying a corrupted value as the corruption grows, with the full record available and the paired field scaled alike, for proteins (\textbf {a}, value decreased; \textbf {b}, increased) and molecules (\textbf {c}, decreased; \textbf {d}, increased). Bands: 95\% Wilson intervals (30--40 tasks per point after excluding runs without a parseable answer). Strips below each panel: fitted factor at which half of runs relay the value, with its 95\% interval; estimates outside the tested range are given as numbers}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {A derivation route raises detection; agreement decides override.} \textbf  {a}, New chemistry fields with the full record, per model: uncorrupted runs raising a concern (crosses); plausible-error runs raising a concern (open circles) and relaying the value (squares); impossible-value runs relaying it (triangles). \textbf  {b}, Molecular weight asked: relay when the corrupted weight agreed with one or with two other fields. \textbf  {c}, Monoisotopic mass asked: relay when the corrupted mass agreed with two other fields or was changed alone. Large markers pool three samples (95\% Wilson intervals); small dots are single samples; numbers give the change in percentage points.}}{22}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:agreement}{{4}{22}{\textbf {A derivation route raises detection; agreement decides override.} \textbf {a}, New chemistry fields with the full record, per model: uncorrupted runs raising a concern (crosses); plausible-error runs raising a concern (open circles) and relaying the value (squares); impossible-value runs relaying it (triangles). \textbf {b}, Molecular weight asked: relay when the corrupted weight agreed with one or with two other fields. \textbf {c}, Monoisotopic mass asked: relay when the corrupted mass agreed with two other fields or was changed alone. Large markers pool three samples (95\% Wilson intervals); small dots are single samples; numbers give the change in percentage points}{figure.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Real, historically corrected UniProt errors.} \textbf  {a}, Relay of the superseded value with the full (filled) and summary (open) record; small dots, repeat samples with the full record. \textbf  {b}, For each of the 79 proteins, the share of six model--format runs relaying the superseded value against the size of the historical change in length, coloured by whether the superseded sequence was longer or shorter than today's; black bars, pooled rate per size bin with 95\% interval. \textbf  {c}, With AlphaFold~DB as second source, residual relay on covered proteins by direction of the revision (three samples pooled). \textbf  {d}, Relay without (open) and with (filled) the second source.}}{23}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:real}{{5}{23}{\textbf {Real, historically corrected UniProt errors.} \textbf {a}, Relay of the superseded value with the full (filled) and summary (open) record; small dots, repeat samples with the full record. \textbf {b}, For each of the 79 proteins, the share of six model--format runs relaying the superseded value against the size of the historical change in length, coloured by whether the superseded sequence was longer or shorter than today's; black bars, pooled rate per size bin with 95\% interval. \textbf {c}, With AlphaFold~DB as second source, residual relay on covered proteins by direction of the revision (three samples pooled). \textbf {d}, Relay without (open) and with (filled) the second source}{figure.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Correcting the record works; showing the conflict does not.} \textbf  {a}, Relay of superseded values on covered proteins under four presentations of the second source (three samples per run; 95\% Wilson intervals; small dots, single samples; counts above). \textbf  {b}, What the relayed answers cited, by a text match on the final answer: a recalled canonical value, the original or observed value shown in the verification note, or neither.}}{24}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:presentation}{{6}{24}{\textbf {Correcting the record works; showing the conflict does not.} \textbf {a}, Relay of superseded values on covered proteins under four presentations of the second source (three samples per run; 95\% Wilson intervals; small dots, single samples; counts above). \textbf {b}, What the relayed answers cited, by a text match on the final answer: a recalled canonical value, the original or observed value shown in the verification note, or neither}{figure.6}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {What turns detection into override.} \textbf  {a--c}, Relay under four system prompts (the registered default; without the instruction to use the tools; with a note that the database contains errors and the sequence or formula is authoritative; with the answer and the record's value asked for as separate fields) on three cells: protein tasks whose full record contradicts the value (\textbf  {a}), the chemistry agreement cell with three fields agreeing with the wrong weight, three samples (\textbf  {b}), and coherent protein errors that nothing in the record contradicts (\textbf  {c}). \textbf  {d}, Repetition versus consistency: relay of a wrong molecular weight alone, with a verbatim repeat that agrees, with a repeat that disagrees, and with a derived field (heavy-atom count) that agrees; 60 compounds, three samples. \textbf  {e}, Memory probe: relay of the superseded value for proteins whose memory held the current (filled) or the superseded (open) length, without a second source (full record) and with the second source shown (three samples). \textbf  {f}, Withholding harness: relay of corrupted numeric fields, withheld when they fail the derivation check (circles), and of identifiers, which the harness does not check (diamonds); impossible and plausible pooled. Markers give relay with 95\% Wilson intervals; lines connect one model.}}{25}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:mechanism}{{7}{25}{\textbf {What turns detection into override.} \textbf {a--c}, Relay under four system prompts (the registered default; without the instruction to use the tools; with a note that the database contains errors and the sequence or formula is authoritative; with the answer and the record's value asked for as separate fields) on three cells: protein tasks whose full record contradicts the value (\textbf {a}), the chemistry agreement cell with three fields agreeing with the wrong weight, three samples (\textbf {b}), and coherent protein errors that nothing in the record contradicts (\textbf {c}). \textbf {d}, Repetition versus consistency: relay of a wrong molecular weight alone, with a verbatim repeat that agrees, with a repeat that disagrees, and with a derived field (heavy-atom count) that agrees; 60 compounds, three samples. \textbf {e}, Memory probe: relay of the superseded value for proteins whose memory held the current (filled) or the superseded (open) length, without a second source (full record) and with the second source shown (three samples). \textbf {f}, Withholding harness: relay of corrupted numeric fields, withheld when they fail the derivation check (circles), and of identifiers, which the harness does not check (diamonds); impossible and plausible pooled. Markers give relay with 95\% Wilson intervals; lines connect one model}{figure.7}{}}
